# ============================================================================
# DriveBase — Agent Rules
# Vehicle Management System for German Auto Dealerships
# ============================================================================
# This file governs ALL AI agent behavior in this repository.
# Rules are MANDATORY and must NEVER be skipped, overridden, or summarized away.
# ============================================================================


# ============================================================================
# 🔒 RULE 0: MANDATORY PRE-WORK CONTEXT LOADING
# ============================================================================
# Before writing ANY code, making ANY modification, or proposing ANY plan,
# you MUST read and internalize the following files IN THIS ORDER:
#
# 1. README.md              — Project overview, tech stack, architecture
# 2. schema.prisma          — Complete data model reference (all entities & relations)
# 3. PROJECT_MAP.md         — File-level map of the codebase and module boundaries
# 4. .agents/skills/        — Scan available skills for the current task (see §2)
# 5. Relevant artifacts     — Check conversation artifacts for prior decisions
#
# DO NOT skip this step even if the task seems trivial.
# DO NOT rely on memory from previous conversations — always re-read.
# If any of these files are empty or missing, note it and proceed with caution.
#
# This rule exists because the codebase has domain-specific conventions
# (German legal documents, multi-tenant isolation, dual auth flows) that
# are easy to violate without full context.


# ============================================================================
# 📁 RULE 0.1: ARTIFACT PERSISTENCE
# ============================================================================
# When you create planning artifacts (implementation plans, task checklists,
# walkthroughs, discovery reports, audit findings, etc.), you MUST also save
# a copy to the project's artifact archive:
#
#   .agents/artifacts/<topic-name>/
#
# Structure each topic folder with descriptive filenames:
#   .agents/artifacts/<topic-name>/<topic>-plan.md
#   .agents/artifacts/<topic-name>/<topic>-tasks.md
#   .agents/artifacts/<topic-name>/<topic>-walkthrough.md
#
# Examples:
#   .agents/artifacts/security-audit/security-audit-plan.md
#   .agents/artifacts/i18n-implementation/i18n-implementation-walkthrough.md
#   .agents/artifacts/vehicle-form-refactor/vehicle-form-refactor-tasks.md
#
# This ensures that prior architectural decisions, completed work, and known
# constraints are preserved across conversations and accessible to future agents.
# DO NOT dump all artifacts in the root — always use a descriptive subdirectory.
# DO NOT change the contents of existing artifact files unless explicitly asked.


# ============================================================================
# 🔄 RULE 0.2: LIVING DOCUMENTATION — KEEP REFERENCE FILES UP TO DATE
# ============================================================================
# The following three files are the project's source of truth for agents and
# developers. After completing work that changes the codebase structure, data
# model, or project capabilities, you MUST update the affected files:
#
# 1. README.md       — Update if: tech stack changes, new features added,
#                       setup steps change, API endpoints added/removed,
#                       environment variables change, or auth flows evolve.
#
# 2. schema.prisma   — Update if: Django models are added, modified, or
#                       deleted. Any change to models.py (new fields, new
#                       models, renamed columns, new relations) MUST be
#                       reflected here to keep it in sync with the ORM.
#
# 3. PROJECT_MAP.md  — Update if: new files/modules are created, files are
#                       renamed or deleted, new pages/components/hooks are
#                       added, API endpoint files change, or the directory
#                       structure is reorganized.
#
# These updates should happen AT THE END of your work, after all code changes
# are verified and working. Do not update them speculatively.
# When in doubt, update — stale docs are worse than a few extra lines.


# ============================================================================
# §1 — PROJECT IDENTITY & DOMAIN
# ============================================================================

## 1.1 What this project is
# DriveBase is a multi-tenant vehicle management platform for used-car dealerships
# in Germany. It manages vehicles, financial transactions, legal entities
# (buyers/sellers), and generates legally-compliant German PDF documents.

## 1.2 Tech stack (never deviate without explicit user approval)
# Backend:  Python 3 · Django 5.2 · Django Ninja (REST API) · SQLite (dev)
# Frontend: React 19 · TypeScript · Vite 7 · TailwindCSS 4 · Radix UI
#           React Router 7 · TanStack Query · TanStack Table
#           React Hook Form + Zod · i18next · Axios · Lucide React
# Auth:     Session-based + CSRF · Manager magic-link · Employee approval-polling
# PDF:      ReportLab · num2words (German)
# i18n:     German (default), English, Turkish, Arabic (RTL)

## 1.3 Multi-tenancy model
# ALL data is scoped to a Business entity. Every query MUST filter by
# business_id. Cross-tenant data leaks are a critical security violation.
# When writing any new API endpoint or query:
#   - Always filter by request.user.business
#   - Never expose raw database IDs in URLs (use internal_id per business)
#   - Verify data ownership before any mutation


